<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Protest and freedom · 1931 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Solidaritätslied</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A German workers' song of the early 1930s by Bertolt Brecht and Hanns Eisler, calling on working people to stand together.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Bertolt Brecht</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Hanns Eisler</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the German workers' movement</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Germany</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>written 1929–1931; first performed 1932</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Protest and freedom</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>In copyright (words by Bertolt Brecht, music by Hanns Eisler (1929–1931))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidaritatslied">Solidaritätslied</a>&quot; (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidarity-polish-trade-union">Solidarity</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>) is a revolutionary workers' song written between 1929 and 1931 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bertolt-brecht">Bertolt Brecht</a> and set to music by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanns-eisler">Hanns Eisler</a>. It was written against the background of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a> and the social problems of industrial society that Brecht explored in his 1932 film Kuhle Wampe, in which the song appears and its melody recurs. Eisler also used it in his orchestral Suite No. 3, Kuhle Wampe, Op. 26, of 1932.</p><p>In 1932 the song was first performed with several working men's choirs, and in the last months of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a> it spread quickly, particularly at sporting events. Brecht wrote two versions of the text. The first is closely tied to the film; the second, better known, was written during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a> and is more abstract and ideological.</p><p>Eisler's setting is in D minor. The opening syllables of the word &quot;Solidarität&quot; are sung on one repeated note, and the last falls a semitone onto a dominant chord; the coda repeats this change and ends on the dominant rather than returning to the tonic, leaving the song open in music as in words. The melody also carries the B–A–C–H motif in its opening bars.</p><p>The text has been translated into many languages. The best-known recording is by Ernst Busch, who also appeared in Kuhle Wampe. In 1961 a group of East German composers, among them Andre Asriel, Fritz Geißler, Günter Kochan and Ruth Zechlin, some of them Eisler's students, wrote joint orchestral variations on it. Frederic Rzewski quoted it in variation 26 of his piano work The People United Will Never Be Defeated! (1975), and its final two bars served as the interval signal of the East German broadcaster Stimme der DDR.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song of workers' solidarity and class struggle, calling working people everywhere to unite; urgent and marching.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords sg-nowords-g" data-say>The words of this song are still in copyright, so the Institute does not print them itself: Genius shows them below, under its own licence.</p><div class="sg-genius"><div class="sg-genius-btns"><button type="button" class="sg-genius-btn" data-genius="6485739" data-what="Lyrics">Show the lyrics from Genius</button></div><div class="sg-genius-frames"></div><p class="sg-genius-note">Genius shows the lyrics in its own frame, under its own licence: nothing loads from Genius until you press a button. <a href="https://genius.com/Ernst-busch-solidaritatslied-lyrics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The song on Genius</a>.</p></div></section>
</div>
